From c6ced5a76cfe89f4d092dd11993e7b9cf2f7f620 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 21:34:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(rlsm): apply zone preprocessing, decode once, select
 Spanish traineddata
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three OCR-path gaps measured against the operator's 12.3k-screenshot corpus,
which the sandbox this branch was written in did not have.

1. Preprocessing was declared and never applied. ZONE_OCR_CONFIG has carried a
   `preprocess` mode per zone since the zone schema was written; both runners
   read the dict, used `psm`, and sent raw crops to Tesseract. FR24 map labels
   are thin antialiased text over a moving basemap — exactly the input
   Tesseract's document-oriented thresholding shreds. A real read from the
   corpus is "be Baya ecibo" for BAYAMON / ARECIBO.

   fr24/rlsm_preprocess.py implements the modes in pure PIL (matching
   rlsm_icons.py): grayscale, upscale, Otsu binarize with the polarity chosen
   per crop — FR24 draws light labels on satellite tiles and dark labels on the
   light basemap, and text is always the minority class.

   Measured over 22 screenshots stride-sampled across every month bucket,
   distinct gazetteer hits per frame:

       label_layer     0.41 -> 0.86   (words recovered 53 -> 136)
       aircraft_card   0.30 -> 0.77   (mean confidence 55.4 -> 60.8)

   Roughly double the usable POI matches on both zones, by different routes:
   the label layer gains recall while its per-word confidence barely moves; the
   already-legible aircraft card gains confidence. Cost is near neutral. A 3x
   upscale was measured and rejected: more words, no more hits, 1.4x the time.
   label_mask and high_contrast are inside each other's noise at this sample
   size, so each zone keeps the mode the schema originally declared rather than
   claiming a winner the data does not support.

2. Every image was decoded four times per pass — Image.open sat inside the zone
   loop, re-reading a 3-5 MB file for each of three zones. Decode once, crop
   three times.

3. No language selection. Every toponym in this corpus is Spanish (Bayamon,
   Anasco, Penuelas, Mayaguez) and the English model has no priors for the
   accented forms. Both runners now use `-l spa+eng` when spa traineddata is
   installed and fall back to eng. engine_version is recorded per observation
   as well, so a later Tesseract 5 re-run stays distinguishable.

Coordinate contract: preprocessing rescales the crop, so word boxes come back
in scaled coordinates. words_from_tesseract_data takes a `scale` and divides it
out before adding the zone offset. Getting this wrong raises nothing — it
doubles every pin coordinate and the affine geocoder fits the wrong points — so
tests/test_rlsm_ocr_preprocessing.py pins the arithmetic (13 tests; 59 pass
with the existing label-extraction suite).

scripts/rlsm_ocr_bench.py is the instrument behind the numbers above:
read-only, resumable, appends to outputs/ocr_bench.jsonl. Re-run it before
changing a zone's mode or scale.

